## The Challenge: Styling Encapsulated Components

By default, **Lit** elements use the **Shadow DOM**, which encapsulates styles. This means styles defined inside a component won't leak out and affect the main page, and most external page styles won't leak in and break the component. This is great for predictability but presents a challenge when you want to allow users to customize your component's appearance.

## Solution: A Public Styling API

To allow users to style your component, you must create an explicit **public styling interface**. This is achieved using two standard browser features.

### Tool 1: CSS Custom Properties

- **Purpose**: To allow customization of individual CSS property values (e.g., colors, fonts, sizes).
- **Implementation**: Inside your component's CSS, use the `var()` function to reference a custom property, providing a fallback value for the default style.
  ```css
  .container {
    /* Uses --my-bg-color if provided, otherwise defaults to white */
    background-color: var(--my-bg-color, white);
    box-shadow: var(--carousel-box-shadow, 0 0 1em 0 rgba(0,0,0,0.25));
  }
  ```
- **Consumption**: The user sets the custom property on the component's host element or any ancestor.
  ```css
  simple-carousel {
    --carousel-box-shadow: none;
  }
  ```

> [!TIP]
> Some CSS properties like `color` and `font-family` are **inheritable** and will automatically pass through the Shadow DOM boundary. For non-text elements like an SVG, you can use the `currentColor` keyword to make them adopt the inherited text color.

### Tool 2: CSS Shadow Parts

- **Purpose**: To expose entire internal elements for unrestricted styling by the user. This is for when changing a single property isn't enough.
- **Implementation**: In your component's template, add the `part` attribute to an internal element.
  ```html
  <div part="container">...</div>
  <button part="nav-button left-button">◀</button>
  ```
- **Consumption**: The user targets the part with the `::part()` pseudo-element selector.
  ```css
  simple-carousel::part(container) {
    border-radius: 0;
    border: 2px solid grey;
  }

  simple-carousel::part(nav-button) {
    display: none; /* Hide all nav buttons */
  }
  ```
- **Forwarding Parts**: If your component uses another custom element that exposes its own parts, you can re-expose them using the `exportparts` attribute.

## Key Takeaways

*   **Prioritize Platform Standards**: Lit embraces browser standards. Both CSS Custom Properties and a Shadow Parts are native web platform features, ensuring long-term compatibility and interoperability.
*   **Document Your API**: A good component library provides clear documentation for its styling API, listing all available custom properties and shadow parts. The Lit `playground-ide` element is a great example of this.

> [!IMPORTANT]
> The video includes a humorous warning against creating auto-playing carousels, as they often provide a poor user experience.
